00:09:47.403 --> 00:09:51.120
@denethweerasinghe
Is the stereotype of Oxford PPE students real?

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.160
Ok now that we have established that Oxbridge&nbsp;
is quite a diverse place and people end up doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:01.980
all kinds of things, let's get into the gossip.
To explain, the Oxford PPE program is a degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:08.040
in Philosophy, politics and economics. The&nbsp;
degree was introduced in the 1920s as a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:13.980
modern version of Classics which was what most&nbsp;
Oxbridge quote unquote “gentlemen” of the 19th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:20.400
century took if they wanted to go into politics&nbsp;
or the Imperial or home civil service. Today PPE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:26.400
is most well known for being the degree that most&nbsp;
future politicians take at Oxford. Harold Wilson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:33.540
Edward Heath, David Cameron, Liz Truss and Rishi&nbsp;
Sunak all took PPE at Oxford. It’s usually future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.520
conservative party members that take PPE at&nbsp;
Oxford rather than others so the degree is often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:44.640
but not exclusively, associated with posh upper&nbsp;
class people who want to go into politics. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:50.400
this stereotype is honestly true. Although,&nbsp;
most people who took PPE that I met weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:54.720
pretentious, most of them were nice and didn’t&nbsp;
act like they were better than everyone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.940
they were just often ambitious, since they wanted&nbsp;
to go into politics, and quite posh, usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:05.280
coming from public schools, but I didn’t find them&nbsp;
to be mean or pretentious or anything. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:10.380
I met more pretentious people studying history&nbsp;
than PPE and the subjects that have the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:15.900
pretentious people are various business degrees.&nbsp;
These are usually taken by kids of parents who own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:21.300
companies so they can party and network with other&nbsp;
rich kids while at Oxford and then be given a job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:26.460
by their parents. Most business students don’t&nbsp;
even really mix with the other students. I lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:31.380
with an MBA student while at Oxford so I had the&nbsp;
privilege to observe these people and I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:36.660
best way to summarize it is this quote from The&nbsp;
Spector, ‘attending a mixer at the Said Business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:41.040
School’, that is what the University of Oxford&nbsp;
Business school is called, ‘is like a United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:46.560
Nations for the 1 percent. There’s the grandson&nbsp;
of a Greek shipping tycoon; the daughter of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:51.300
man who owns a Lithuanian bank; a guy whose&nbsp;
thesis is funded by the Qatari government;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:57.120
the Saudi brothers who jet in and out weekly from&nbsp;
the Gulf’ and so on. I have to emphasize though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:02.400
that in my experience none of them were mean&nbsp;
to me and not all of them were pretentious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:06.900
but when I found a non-pretentious person it&nbsp;
was more of a surprise rather than the norm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:11.460
and I also wouldn’t call any of them&nbsp;
dumb they were just often out of touch.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:17.730
---------------------------------------------------

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:23.460
@tacomusical1404
Could you answer,how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:24.840
to be a self-trained historian?

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:28.980
Read a lot of history books and historical&nbsp;
journal articles about a specific topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:33.240
you want to learn about. After you have done&nbsp;
that you can start learning historical theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.780
that is the theories behind the various&nbsp;
ways historians approach history.&nbsp;

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:40.920
In my experience, people would talk about&nbsp;
how this book about Czechoslovak history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:46.200
was written in a very whiggish way or this book&nbsp;
on early medieval Slavic history was written in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:50.460
a very postmodernist way, and at the time I&nbsp;
didn’t know enough about Czechoslovak history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:55.080
or Medieval Slavic history to understand what&nbsp;
history they were talking about, let alone the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.080 --> 00:13:00.000
historical theories they were mentioning.&nbsp;
So my advice is to first just go on google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.560
search by books, and type in whatever topic&nbsp;
you want to learn about and read a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:08.700
those books. After that it wouldn’t hurt to&nbsp;
look at the primary sources the books are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:12.900
referencing. Ones you have read many of the&nbsp;
books and seen some of the primary sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:17.340
you will notice that even though it’s the same&nbsp;
topic and largely the same primary sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:22.200
unless something new has been discovered, all the&nbsp;
books have a different approach towards the topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:27.420
and what you’re noticing is the various historical&nbsp;
theories that the historian who wrote those books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:32.520
are using to approach the same historical topic.&nbsp;
Once you get to that point you can actually start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:37.080
reading about historical theory, by buying&nbsp;
and reading the various books on historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.660
theory (History: An Introduction to Theory, Method&nbsp;
and Practice), and since you already read a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:44.820
history books beforehand you can understand better&nbsp;
what the historical theories actually look like in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:50.100
practice. Of course, this wouldn’t really work&nbsp;
with niche topics, like there aren’t many books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:55.140
on early medieval guilds for example, so it’s&nbsp;
better to start with broader well documented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:59.640
topics were there are multiple books written&nbsp;
in multiple different ways and than you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:05.460
go into these nicher topics and see like ‘ahh in&nbsp;
this one niche topic there’s only this one book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:10.560
that used this historical approach.’
So, yeah, hopefully that made sense.

