history books and articles and getting those books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:30.660
and articles is 100% prohibitively expensive. For&nbsp;
example, Sources of Slavic Pre-Christian Religion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:37.380
costs 180 euros! Don’t get me wrong, the writers&nbsp;
of this book did a lot of work tracking down all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:41.460
the sources, translating them, and giving&nbsp;
context to them and it is the best book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:45.660
out there if you want to understand what we&nbsp;
know and more importantly what we don’t know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:51.720
about Slavic paganism. But my god no one with&nbsp;
institutional financial support will be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:57.060
to justify buying this. And I didn’t pick some&nbsp;
random book that happens to be expensive, most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:03.120
history books which aren’t pop-history books, are&nbsp;
freaking expensive. The multi volume set of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:10.140
Cambridge History of Russia costs 111 pounds if&nbsp;
you buy the paperback and over 300 pounds if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:15.840
buy the hardcover. Greek Historical Inscriptions&nbsp;
478-404 BC cost 180 pounds, well now you can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:21.600
it in paperback for 35 pounds but that’s only&nbsp;
from 2020, I had to pay 180 pounds for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:29.700
Journals also aren’t any better. The Journal of&nbsp;
Slavic Military Studies costs 137 pounds a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:36.480
and if you just want to read one article, no&nbsp;
problem, just pay 37 pounds for 48 hours access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:42.960
for one 30 paged article. It’s ridiculous.
No wonder academia is disconnected from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:48.000
average person when no average person can&nbsp;
afford to read academic publications. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:53.040
is it all so expensive? Well, I haven’t done any&nbsp;
research into it but my guess would be that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:58.800
book and journal publishers make money off of&nbsp;
institutional subscriptions. If they make books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:04.620
and subscriptions, which universities, libraries,&nbsp;
and research institutions need to buy, expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:09.480
they can justify charging them millions&nbsp;
of dollars. And this is where publishers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:16.500
make money, £180m are spent annually by&nbsp;
UK universities on journal subscriptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:21.660
alone. These are the customers publishers&nbsp;
are aiming for, not individual researchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:27.660
so they make their publications expensive knowing&nbsp;
full well not many individual people will pay for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:33.240
it but universities, libraries, and research&nbsp;
institutions will pay millions for it. At least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:37.080
that’s what I think but I am sure there’s&nbsp;
more to the story that I am not aware of.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:42.480
This whole thing is, however, a problem for&nbsp;
individuals who want to get into history. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:47.520
only way an individual can circumvent&nbsp;
it, is by getting a library membership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:52.800
and hope that the library near you is well&nbsp;
stocked, has enough money to get new books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:57.780
and has fairly good coverage of paid journal&nbsp;
articles. Some libraries do have all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.780 --> 00:17:02.700
especially in big cities, and they are amazing&nbsp;
institutions and I recommend paying for a library&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:08.160
membership because they are essential for a&nbsp;
modern society, but not everyone has access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:13.580
libraries like these at which point you’re sadly&nbsp;
stuck buying very expensive books and articles.

00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:14.580
----------------------------------------------------

00:17:14.580 --> 00:17:18.420
@jordanhicken7812
Is going for your PhD in History&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:22.800
something that you would recommend to someone&nbsp;
who is passionate about history and teaching?

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:27.480
I am gonna say what every history professor&nbsp;
I talked to about this told me, and that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:32.940
on average NO. But there are exceptions and of&nbsp;
course you always have to consider your personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:39.240
situation. On average, however, why most people&nbsp;
say no is because the only job in the world that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:44.700
requires a PhD in history is to be an academic&nbsp;
historian so the employment market isn’t very big.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:48.480
Basically, when it comes to a PhD in&nbsp;
history, the fact that you really only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:53.100
need it for one specific thing means that&nbsp;
you’re better off going into the private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:57.420
sector with just a masters. When you try&nbsp;
to go into the private sector with a PhD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.420 --> 00:18:02.940
employers often think you’re overqualified. Which&nbsp;
is stupid because a PhD shows that you’re capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:07.320
of doing extensive learning on your own and&nbsp;
have the capability to learn complex things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:12.600
but that’s not how most employers see it.&nbsp;
Therefore, a PhD in history is only really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:16.740
useful in the academic sector. The problem&nbsp;
with the academic sector is that academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:22.320
positions are few and far apart and, because&nbsp;
very rarely are new positions added, you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:27.840
often waiting for old tenure professors to retire&nbsp;
or since a lot of professors aren’t retiring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:34.200
even die. Meaning whether you get an academic job&nbsp;
is extremely luck based. Also, in the UK, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:39.060
there’s more PhD holders that want academic&nbsp;
positions than there are actual positions, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:44.100
current system of academic employment is pretty&nbsp;
broken. The exploitation of non permanent position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:49.200
academics is part of the reason why the current&nbsp;
university marking boycott is happening in the UK.

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:57.540
There’s also a problem with prestige. When&nbsp;
it comes to learning and making a well made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:05.520
PhD thesis in history, the supervisor is often&nbsp;
far more important than the institution you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:11.340
at. However, after you graduate, sadly a lot&nbsp;
of employers don’t care about that. From my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:17.280
experience, someone with fairly mediocre research&nbsp;
and grades who has a doctorate from Oxbridge or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:22.800
an Ivy League university is far more likely to be&nbsp;
employed than someone who is smarter but is from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:24.004
a less prestigious university. For example, if you&nbsp;
look at most university history departments in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.004 --> 00:19:26.280
UK, most of the professors there have Oxbridge&nbsp;
degrees. I even had a friend who did a masters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:30.000
with me at Oxford and didn’t get into a funded&nbsp;
doctorate at Oxbridge, Edinburgh, and Durham,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.520
but he did get a fully funded PhD offer at the&nbsp;
University of Southampton, but even though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:40.500
was fully funded, he told me it wasn’t worth&nbsp;
taking it because trying to find employment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:46.080
after finishing a PhD at not so prestigious&nbsp;
university will be very hard. And even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:51.240
he really wanted to do a PhD which continued on&nbsp;
his masters research which was very interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:56.520
he went into the private sector instead because&nbsp;
he was convinced that without a PhD from a top UK&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.520 --> 00:20:01.200
university it would be very hard for him to&nbsp;
find an academic job afterwards. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:06.060
this part is all just my experience and what I&nbsp;
have seen and heard from people in the sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:08.220
I have not done any extensive research into this.

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:17.700
Now all of this is based on money which&nbsp;
contrary to what some people on the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:22.620
or in the government believe isn’t the only&nbsp;
driving force for human beings. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:26.280
in our current economic system you&nbsp;
need money to survive but there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:29.760
other factors that play into why&nbsp;
someone would want to do a PhD.

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:34.620
Doing a PhD in history, I would argue, is&nbsp;
very intellectually fulfilling and teaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:39.060
you a lot of valuable lessons about doing&nbsp;
proper research and how to work on things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:48.840
that take years to finish. You’re also doing&nbsp;
a PhD in history because you’re passionate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:52.740
about the subject and there’s many other&nbsp;
people that are doing the same thing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:58.020
that you will meet during your PhD and make&nbsp;
lifelong connections with. And from anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:01.980
we all know that the power of friendship&nbsp;
is the strongest thing in the universe.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.920
Jokes aside, the bottom line is this.&nbsp;

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:08.640
If you’re a very financially driven&nbsp;
person you shouldn’t do a PhD in history.&nbsp;

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:13.500
If you are in a financially unstable place&nbsp;
you probably shouldn’t do a PhD in history.&nbsp;

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:18.540
If you, however, managed to secure funding&nbsp;
for a PhD, then perhaps consider doing it.&nbsp;

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:22.980
If you are very passionate about the subject&nbsp;
you will research, also consider doing it.&nbsp;

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:27.480
If you manage to get into a prestigious&nbsp;
institution, you should consider doing it.

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:33.480
In the end I can’t tell you outright&nbsp;
whether to do or not to do a PhD in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:39.000
but hopefully I have provided some information&nbsp;
that can help you make a more educated decision.

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:43.200
--------------------------------------------------

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:54.600
@nolancrowe3805
I'd be interested to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:57.000
hear generally about your experiences in academia.&nbsp;

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.300
What are your thoughts on the current&nbsp;
state of history as a discipline?&nbsp;

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:04.920
Could you speak to current trends within&nbsp;
history as a discipline or academia generally?&nbsp;

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:07.920
What do you think of the types of&nbsp;
people you run into in academia?

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:13.320
Alright, so we shall combine all the things&nbsp;
I have said in the previous two responses for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:17.700
this response. I will continue to talk about&nbsp;
academic history in the UK because that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:21.300
what I know the best, so keep in mind even&nbsp;
though there are many similarities between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:25.980
academic departments or academic history in&nbsp;
other countries there are also differences.

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:32.160
First, most academics in the UK are male, 72% to&nbsp;
be exact (80% when it comes to History). Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:37.380
is probably still a remnant of the fact that some&nbsp;
institutions like Magdalena College at Cambridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:45.000
only allowed female students in 1988. Also, most&nbsp;
institutions hire early career academics based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:50.760
on an annual contract rather than give you a fixed&nbsp;
job which means women don’t get paid maternity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:55.380
leave and often just lose their contracts&nbsp;
if they have a baby. As one woman put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:59.160
‘It was very weird going on maternity&nbsp;
leave knowing that that was the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:05.460
my job … It’s truly my dream job. And it was a&nbsp;
completely irrational thing, but for a while I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:09.660
resented the baby because it felt like I&nbsp;
had been forced to give up a job I love.’

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:15.480
Also, as we have discussed, getting into history&nbsp;
as an individual can be quite expensive, and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:20.640
often not recommended for people to do a PhD in&nbsp;
history unless they are in a financially stable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:26.520
place. Since full PhD funding for history is very&nbsp;
rare, and everyone in academia must have a PhD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:31.020
this means most academic historians,&nbsp;
especially ones in permanent positions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:36.000
come from a fairly privileged background. As&nbsp;
a journalist report by The Economist states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:41.940
in the UK, due to various reasons, ‘it is far&nbsp;
easier to get a permanent academic job if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:46.920
are rich than if you are poor.’ Plus, as we&nbsp;
already discussed, a lot of early academics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:51.960
are being quite heavily exploited as permanent&nbsp;
positions are very limited and often held by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:56.700
older academics. This means that a lot of&nbsp;
academic history in the UK is controlled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.700 --> 00:24:13.080
older upper middle class men. The kind of people&nbsp;
that don’t deal well with change, or technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:17.400
and I am not talking about political leanings&nbsp;
here, I am talking solely about history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:22.020
and trying to implement changes in the field&nbsp;
to keep up with the ever changing world.

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:27.540
A lot of academics I met are set in their&nbsp;
ways, hard to convince to work on new and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:32.700
different projects, and usually quite defensive&nbsp;
about any kind of change. I cannot blame them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:37.140
for the latter reason, academic history has&nbsp;
always been attacked by a variety of groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:41.340
especially governments that don’t like historians&nbsp;
who talk about history that paints them in a bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:45.960
light. Not to mention that funding for history&nbsp;
has always been limited as people and governments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:50.160
often don’t find it valuable and so don’t&nbsp;
fund it as much as the sciences. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:56.340
being quite defensive of your job especially when&nbsp;
your source of funding is limited is expected.

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:23.100
On the other hand, however, as my paper&nbsp;
discussed, link to it in the pinned comment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:28.560
academic history has become quite disconnected&nbsp;
from the public and some of the research projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:34.560
that people are doing can be hard to defend. Of&nbsp;
course in a perfect world I think you should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:39.660
able to do any kind of research because it’s all&nbsp;
valuable, but we do not live in a perfect world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.660 --> 00:25:45.720
and when funding and time is limited it’s hard&nbsp;
to defend someone doing a funded PhD thesis on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.075 --> 00:25:54.295
I have met academics who lived their entire&nbsp;
lives without considering the greater value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.295 --> 00:26:00.415
of their work to society other than saying it’s&nbsp;
all intrinsically valuable. There are 60 year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.415 --> 00:26:05.155
old academics that literally since undergrad&nbsp;
have not left the university and have only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.155 --> 00:26:10.615
written articles and books that are so niche&nbsp;
and boring only a handful of other academics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.615 --> 00:26:15.355
have ever read them. That is why I can see&nbsp;
why a lot of people from the outside look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.355 --> 00:26:19.795
at academic history and then decide history&nbsp;
is useless and don’t give it much funding.

00:26:19.795 --> 00:26:24.475
Therefore, there needs to be some kind of&nbsp;
a reimagining of how academic history is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.475 --> 00:26:30.055
conducted and what are its goals, because clearly&nbsp;
just saying it’s all intrinsically valuable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.055 --> 00:26:36.055
no matter how true it may be, doesn’t resonate&nbsp;
with most of the population. Don’t get me wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.055 --> 00:26:41.635
I often go to bat for academic humanities because&nbsp;
current attacks calling it useless are just not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.635 --> 00:26:46.675
true, but I am also aware that those attacks&nbsp;
don’t come from nowhere and something needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.675 --> 00:26:52.255
be done to address the source of this sentiment.&nbsp;
Academics need to find another way, other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.255 --> 00:26:57.355
saying it’s intrinsically valuable, to showcase&nbsp;
the value of academic history to the public and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.355 --> 00:27:03.355
not be stuck in their ivory towers, otherwise&nbsp;
the current attacks on academia will only worsen.

00:27:03.355 --> 00:27:03.882
