00:28:24.895 --> 00:28:29.112
so yeah, it usually all depends on&nbsp;
what I am doing or reading at the time.

00:28:29.112 --> 00:28:29.145
---------------------------------

00:28:29.145 --> 00:28:31.375
@NotShady9
Who is your favourite historical figure?

00:28:31.375 --> 00:28:35.995
Honestly, probably Masaryk, not because&nbsp;
he was perfect or without flaws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.995 --> 00:28:40.915
no one is and he certainly wasn’t, but his&nbsp;
life's trajectory is just interesting to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.915 --> 00:28:44.995
It’s fascinating to me that he taught&nbsp;
himself multiple languages as a kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:44.995 --> 00:28:50.275
or the fact that at various points in his life&nbsp;
he flirted with pan-Slavism, Habsburg Monarchism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.275 --> 00:28:55.675
and republicanism, or the fact that as a leader he&nbsp;
lived through a very tumultuous time and managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.675 --> 00:29:01.915
to stay relatively free of controversy. On the&nbsp;
other hand he did have anger issues and he did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.915 --> 00:29:07.495
publicly forsook his morals a couple of times and&nbsp;
engaged in populist anti-semetic and anti-german&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:07.495 --> 00:29:12.775
politics because he thought he had to in order to&nbsp;
secure a strong nation, but he never followed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.775 --> 00:29:17.875
on these statements and even often contradicted&nbsp;
them with his actions or later apologized for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.875 --> 00:29:23.395
them, so he was certainly not perfect and he&nbsp;
deserves to be called out. With that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.395 --> 00:29:28.135
I think he was an interesting guy, living at an&nbsp;
interesting time, doing some interesting things.

00:29:28.135 --> 00:29:28.734
---------------------------------

00:29:28.734 --> 00:29:29.935
@adisor906
What's the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.935 --> 00:29:32.455
politically biased history book you've ever read?

00:29:32.455 --> 00:29:40.435
Edward Long’s 1774 History of Jamaica. This book&nbsp;
was so racist that it’s honestly impressive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.435 --> 00:29:46.795
Long basically wanted to institutionalize slavery&nbsp;
based on race as a “natural order” of things and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.795 --> 00:29:51.715
through history he tried to very graphically&nbsp;
showcase how Africans were a different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.715 --> 00:29:56.755
inferior race and, therefore, should&nbsp;
be slaves. Even for the 18th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.755 --> 00:30:00.895
a lot of people at the time were surprised just&nbsp;
how racist this book was when it was published.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.895 --> 00:30:07.735
Another really bad book was A History of England&nbsp;
by C.R.L. Fletcher published in 1911 that I quoted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.735 --> 00:30:12.835
in my Oxford and Empire video. The book was quite&nbsp;
racist in order to justify the British Empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.835 --> 00:30:18.535
What makes that book particularly bad though is&nbsp;
that it was made to be used in primary schools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.535 --> 00:30:23.815
and in some parts of England it was used in&nbsp;
primary schools all the way until the 1950s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.815 --> 00:30:29.875
I am sure there are many other books that I have&nbsp;
read over the times that are quite bad but those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.875 --> 00:30:34.195
two stood out to me so much when I read them that&nbsp;
they’re just the first ones that come to mind.

00:30:34.195 --> 00:30:34.646
----------------------------

00:30:34.646 --> 00:30:35.695
@FencingFella
What are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.695 --> 00:30:38.575
myths that Slovak schools teach&nbsp;
as if they were our history?

00:30:38.575 --> 00:30:45.175
There’s a lot of myths, most notably when it&nbsp;
comes to 20th century history there’s a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.175 --> 00:30:46.315
whitewashing (deliberately attempting to conceal&nbsp;
unpleasant or incriminating facts) happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.315 --> 00:30:50.455
but the one thing I hate the most is&nbsp;
unsurprisingly connected to medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.455 --> 00:30:54.475
history. What I am going to talk about is a&nbsp;
mistake almost every European country does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.475 --> 00:30:59.035
not just Slovakia, but the question asked&nbsp;
about Slovakia so I will talk about Slovakia.

00:30:59.035 --> 00:31:05.095
So, basically the 9th century Great Moravia was&nbsp;
formed when the duchy of Moravia invaded and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.095 --> 00:31:10.915
conquered the duchy of Nitra. Now this 9th century&nbsp;
Great Moravia is taught in Slovak schools as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.915 --> 00:31:17.575
having already people who identified as ethnically&nbsp;
Slovak and Czech based on the two duchies i.e.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.575 --> 00:31:22.435
people living in the Moravian duchy were Czech and&nbsp;
people living in the Nitra duchy were Slovak. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.435 --> 00:31:26.575
idea of some kind of a Moravian ethnicity&nbsp;
separate from Czech was never introduced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.575 --> 00:31:31.375
at least when I was in school, and Bohemia&nbsp;
was also often incorporated under the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.375 --> 00:31:36.595
Moravian Czech identity. The problem is that&nbsp;
discussing early medieval ethnicity as if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.595 --> 00:31:42.355
was the same as our modern concept of ethnicity is&nbsp;
at best inappropriate and at worst just outright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.355 --> 00:31:47.515
incorrect. Back then ethnicity wasn’t seen as&nbsp;
an important identifier to a person as it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:47.515 --> 00:31:52.375
to us today. People didn’t care as much about what&nbsp;
ethnicity you were as they did about what religion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.375 --> 00:31:57.895
you were, what legal status you had, what was your&nbsp;
occupation, what leader you followed, etc. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.895 --> 00:32:03.475
why multiethnic kingdoms in the middle ages were&nbsp;
so common, and why prosecution was almost always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.475 --> 00:32:09.715
based on religion rather than ethnicity. Plus,&nbsp;
the idea of a distinct Slovak ethnicity didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.715 --> 00:32:14.515
even exist until the early modern period, before&nbsp;
then the people living in the northern part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.515 --> 00:32:19.975
Kingdom of Hungary that spoke a Slavic language&nbsp;
were in documents just called ‘the Slavs’. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.975 --> 00:32:25.555
why talking about the Slavs of the Nitra duchy&nbsp;
as if they were somehow Slovak back then and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:25.555 --> 00:32:30.835
identified as Slovak or as a distinct ethnically&nbsp;
separate Slavic group from all the other western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:30.835 --> 00:32:36.235
Slavs is just inaccurate. However, part of&nbsp;
an ethnic identity is to have an established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:36.235 --> 00:32:42.055
historical narrative for your ethnicity so, if we&nbsp;
want to keep the modern idea of ethnicity we kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:42.055 --> 00:32:47.635
of have to teach about early medieval kingdoms&nbsp;
in terms of having ethnic continuity to today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:47.635 --> 00:32:52.975
and I don’t think the world is ready to abandon&nbsp;
the modern idea of ethnicity any time soon.

00:32:52.975 --> 00:32:53.380
------------------------------

00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:54.535
@TheRageng
What's your take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.535 --> 00:32:57.475
on the importance of the Slovak uprising of 1848?

00:32:57.475 --> 00:33:03.175
This is a great follow up on the previous question&nbsp;
and how ethnicity wasn’t as important as it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.175 --> 00:33:08.035
now. When you look the uprisings in Austria&nbsp;
in 1848, especially the ones in Slovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.035 --> 00:33:13.615
not only did the quote “Slovak” uprisings have&nbsp;
a lot of people from today’s Czechia in it who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.615 --> 00:33:18.775
we would call today Czech, but they also were&nbsp;
quite disorganized and there were many Slovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.775 --> 00:33:23.935
peasants that fought on the Hungarian side.&nbsp;
The former point shows just how incomplete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.935 --> 00:33:29.035
the creation of a distinct Slovak ethnic group&nbsp;
was at this point and that the idea that Slovaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.035 --> 00:33:33.235
were ethnically separate from Czechs wasn’t&nbsp;
fully developed yet, and the latter point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:33.235 --> 00:33:38.335
shows that the official narrative of a united&nbsp;
Slovak rebellion which wanted autonomy for its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.335 --> 00:33:43.375
ethnic group is just one part of a much greater&nbsp;
story. Yes, there were people like the ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.375 --> 00:33:48.115
in Myjava that fought for ethnic autonomy, but&nbsp;
there were many Slovak peasants that fought in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.115 --> 00:33:53.455
the Hungarian armies which were fighting for the&nbsp;
end of feudal peasantry. As Dusan Kovac states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.455 --> 00:33:58.135
‘Slovak peasants were primarily concerned&nbsp;
with the abolition of feudal subjection and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.135 --> 00:34:02.455
not wider political agenda. Where this&nbsp;
issue connected with the national idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:02.455 --> 00:34:07.075
many Slovak peasants were persuaded to join&nbsp;
the ranks of the Slovak rebels. In regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.075 --> 00:34:12.415
where Magyar revolutionaries had begun to&nbsp;
agitate first, Slovak peasants joined them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.415 --> 00:34:17.875
and fought in the ranks of the Magyar national&nbsp;
guards.’ This nuance has been lost in most modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:17.875 --> 00:34:24.295
Slovak retellings of the story and today the&nbsp;
1848 uprising is remembered as ethnic Slovaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.295 --> 00:34:31.195
fighting for our ethnic autonomy. When in reality&nbsp;
a lot, although of course not all, Slovak peasants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.195 --> 00:34:36.235
just didn’t want to be peasants anymore and they&nbsp;
didn’t really care if that was in a Slovak state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.235 --> 00:34:41.575
a Hungarian state or an Austrian state. So&nbsp;
what’s the importance of the uprising, well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.575 --> 00:34:48.595
the 1848 uprisings directly led to the 1849 land&nbsp;
reform in Austria which ended feudal subjection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:48.595 --> 00:34:54.535
so it was very important, it just wasn’t important&nbsp;
in the way we today in Slovakia think it was.

00:34:54.535 --> 00:34:54.907
-------------------------------

00:34:54.907 --> 00:34:56.335
@history.crusaider
What is your opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.335 --> 00:34:59.575
on Ancient Greek civilizations&nbsp;
and their influence on the world?

00:35:00.175 --> 00:35:04.375
Greek influence on Europe, and due to&nbsp;
European Empires also the rest of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.375 --> 00:35:09.595
is extremely large. Throughout history&nbsp;
Europeans time and time again referred back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.595 --> 00:35:14.455
to ancient Greek and Roman philosophy, art,&nbsp;
architecture, history and more. From modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.455 --> 00:35:19.195
philosophers and historians like Foucault&nbsp;
to renaissance ones like Machiavely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:19.195 --> 00:35:24.475
medieval ones like Albertus Magnus, they all&nbsp;
reference ancient Rome and Greece. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:24.475 --> 00:35:29.695
Roman is not the same as Greek but Romans&nbsp;
did adopt a lot from the Greeks and so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.695 --> 00:35:34.915
historians talk about a not singularly Greek or&nbsp;
Roman legacy in Europe but a Greco-Roman one.

00:35:34.915 --> 00:35:40.615
Why have, however, Europeans been so obsessed&nbsp;
with Greco-Roman ideas, heritage and more?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.615 --> 00:35:46.555
Why do we still build classical style buildings,&nbsp;
still reference Plato and Herodotus, make plays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:46.555 --> 00:35:52.135
movies, and video games about Greek mythology&nbsp;
and more? Perhaps it’s because we have so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.135 --> 00:35:57.715
still remaining from the Greeks, our alphabet,&nbsp;
our sciences and math, our political ideas like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:57.715 --> 00:36:03.775
democracy, and even Christianity has many Greek&nbsp;
aspects. The New Testament was written in Greek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:03.775 --> 00:36:07.735
the word Bible just means ‘the books’&nbsp;
in ancient Greek, and Platonism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.735 --> 00:36:13.915
with its ideas like the theory of the soul, was&nbsp;
a huge influence on christian theology. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.915 --> 00:36:19.375
the earliest ideas of European political theory&nbsp;
come from ancient Greece and so does the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.375 --> 00:36:26.215
citizenship, because it was in the Greek polis&nbsp;
that politics were first recorded and people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.215 --> 00:36:33.535
identified as citizens of a specific citystate.&nbsp;
Of course, Europeans and also the ancient Greeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.535 --> 00:36:39.955
and Romans didn’t develop all this in a vacuum and&nbsp;
there were influences from Egypt, Persia, India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.955 --> 00:36:45.055
etc. but, it was the Greeks and later Romans&nbsp;
not others, that specifically influenced our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:45.055 --> 00:36:51.115
corner of the world the most and so it’s their&nbsp;
philosophy, art, architecture, science, math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.115 --> 00:36:56.875
etc. that are still present today in much of our&nbsp;
language, politics, science and more. With that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.875 --> 00:37:03.055
said, this influence also includes bad ideas, like&nbsp;
the Greco-Roman idea of a very strong patriarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.055 --> 00:37:08.515
which is something we still are trying to shake&nbsp;
to this day. However, because there still is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.515 --> 00:37:13.255
much in our modern European world that can be&nbsp;
traced back to the Greeks and Romans it perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:13.255 --> 00:37:18.115
should not be surprising that we still refer back&nbsp;
to them so much. If you want to know more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.115 --> 00:37:23.275
this topic there’s a good book on the subject&nbsp;
called The Legacy of Greece : a new appraisal.

00:37:23.275 --> 00:37:27.955
My personal interest in Ancient Greece comes&nbsp;
from purely the fact that I want to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.955 --> 00:37:33.055
the history, and historical context that&nbsp;
all these ideas we talked about originated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:33.055 --> 00:37:37.855
from. I also find it funny how a lot of the&nbsp;
politics of the time are quite similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.855 --> 00:37:43.015
our modern politics even though they're&nbsp;
2500 years apart. It helps me, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:43.015 --> 00:37:48.355
realize many important things applicable to&nbsp;
today. For example, it helped me realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.355 --> 00:37:53.815
democracy is quite fragile and that if left&nbsp;
unprotected people like the conservatives of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.815 --> 00:38:00.055
the Areopagus can exploit certain imperfect&nbsp;
systems within democracy to take control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.055 --> 00:38:05.575
of the state. Similar to what some people&nbsp;
have or are trying to do in our modern times.

